{
 "ep": 5,
 "total": 140,
 "title": "PostgreSQL Processes: Postmaster, Backend and Workers",
 "phase": "Foundations",
 "lines": [
  {
   "say": "Why does PostgreSQL create multiple processes?",
   "cap": "Why does PostgreSQL create *multiple* *processes?*"
  },
  {
   "say": "This is episode five of the PostgreSQL DBA series. Today: the process map of a real server.",
   "cap": "Episode 5: the *process* *map* of a real server."
  },
  {
   "say": "PostgreSQL uses a process per user model. Every client connects to exactly one backend process.",
   "cap": "*Process per user:* each client gets *one* *backend.*"
  },
  {
   "say": "We can't know how many clients will connect, so a supervisor process waits for them: the postmaster.",
   "cap": "A supervisor waits for clients: the *postmaster.*",
   "cue": "postmaster"
  },
  {
   "say": "It listens on a TCP port, and for every connection request it spawns a new backend process.",
   "cap": "Each new connection: the postmaster *spawns* a *backend.*",
   "cue": "spawns"
  },
  {
   "say": "All these processes cooperate through shared memory and semaphores.",
   "cap": "Processes cooperate through *shared* *memory* and *semaphores.*",
   "cue": "shared"
  },
  {
   "say": "Let's see them on a real Linux server. List every process owned by the postgres user.",
   "cap": "Live: list the *postgres* user's *processes.*"
  },
  {
   "say": "The first line is the postmaster, with the options it was started with. Output trimmed.",
   "cap": "First line: the *postmaster* and its start options."
  },
  {
   "say": "Next come background processes it launched: checkpointer, background writer, WAL writer and two launchers.",
   "cap": "Then *background* *processes:* checkpointer, writers, launchers."
  },
  {
   "say": "The last line is a client backend: user, database, client host and activity. Here: idle.",
   "cap": "Client backend: *user,* *database,* *host,* *activity.*",
   "cue": "idle"
  },
  {
   "say": "The catch: not every process exists on every server. It depends on settings and role.",
   "cap": "Not every process exists on *every* *server.*"
  },
  {
   "say": "The autovacuum launcher runs only when autovacuum is on, which is the default.",
   "cap": "*Autovacuum* *launcher:* only when autovacuum = *on.*"
  },
  {
   "say": "The archiver is tied to archive_mode. On my test server, it appeared after I turned archive_mode on and restarted.",
   "cap": "*Archiver:* appeared after *archive_mode* = on + restart."
  },
  {
   "say": "With streaming replication, you see a walsender on the primary and a walreceiver on the standby.",
   "cap": "Replication: *walsender* on primary, *walreceiver* on standby.",
   "cue": "walreceiver"
  },
  {
   "say": "Parallel workers come and go with parallel queries, from a pool set by max_worker_processes, eight by default.",
   "cap": "*Parallel* *workers:* pool from *max_worker_processes* (8)."
  },
  {
   "say": "Check your server: select the process ID and backend type from pg_stat_activity.",
   "cap": "Check yours: *pid* and *backend_type* from *pg_stat_activity.*"
  },
  {
   "say": "On my PostgreSQL sixteen test server: six rows, one client backend. The postmaster itself is not listed.",
   "cap": "PostgreSQL 16: *6* *rows.* The *postmaster* is not listed."
  },
  {
   "say": "Now a common mistake: ending a stuck session with kill minus nine.",
   "cap": "Common mistake: *kill -9* on a stuck session."
  },
  {
   "say": "On a test server, the postmaster logged the kill, then terminated every other active server process.",
   "cap": "Postmaster terminated *every* *other* *session* too."
  },
  {
   "say": "Then it reinitialized and ran recovery, because the dead process possibly corrupted shared memory.",
   "cap": "Then *crash* *recovery:* shared memory *possibly* *corrupted.*",
   "cue": "recovery"
  },
  {
   "say": "The safe way is pg_terminate_backend with the process ID. In my test, only that session ended.",
   "cap": "Safe way: *pg_terminate_backend(pid).* Only that session ends."
  },
  {
   "say": "DBA takeaway: every connection is a full process, and a parallel worker costs about as much as another session.",
   "cap": "Every connection is a *process.* So is every *parallel* *worker.*"
  },
  {
   "say": "Recap: one postmaster, one backend per connection, and helper processes that depend on your settings.",
   "cap": "Recap: *postmaster,* *one* *backend* per connection, *helpers.*"
  },
  {
   "say": "Check them in pg_stat_activity, and end sessions with pg_terminate_backend, not kill minus nine.",
   "cap": "Check *pg_stat_activity.* Use *pg_terminate_backend,* not kill -9."
  },
  {
   "say": "Next up: cluster, instance, database and schema. Follow so you don't miss it, and send this to a friend who's learning PostgreSQL.",
   "cap": "Next up: *cluster,* *database,* *schema.* *Follow* and share."
  }
 ],
